WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.069 --> 00:00:07.750
Nearly everything we buy is made overseas.

00:00:07.750 --> 00:00:12.349
No matter where you live, it’s likely that
the majority of products you use on a daily

00:00:12.349 --> 00:00:16.480
basis are made outside your own country’s
borders.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:21.020
Even at the supermarket it’s common to see
fresh food that was grown or caught on the

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:22.929
other side of the world.

00:00:22.929 --> 00:00:25.990
Not long ago, this would have been unheard
of.

00:00:25.990 --> 00:00:28.369
Everything had to be produced close to the
consumer.

00:00:28.369 --> 00:00:32.379
It just wasn’t profitable to ship goods
over long distances.

00:00:32.379 --> 00:00:36.430
Only luxury and specialty items were shipped
from overseas.

00:00:36.430 --> 00:00:40.090
Very few commonplace items were produced more
than a couple hundred miles away from the

00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:41.590
final market.

00:00:41.590 --> 00:00:47.890
That all changed on April 26, 1956, when the
Ideal-X, a converted World War Two tanker

00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:50.130
left Newark, New Jersey on its maiden journey.

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:54.850
What was so special about this journey was
that it was the first time in history that

00:00:54.850 --> 00:00:59.570
a ship had its cargo packed into containers,
rather than just loosely placed throughout

00:00:59.570 --> 00:01:00.900
its holds.

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:06.930
This seems like a simple and uninfluential
concept, but this idea changed our world.

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:13.700
Before the Ideal-X, cargo was brought to port
in trucks and loaded onto ships piece-by-piece.

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:18.890
Whisky and rice and hammers and everything
was packed tightly in the hold, and the whole

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:22.320
loading process took more than a week.

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:27.920
This technique, known as break bulk cargo,
dated back to the time of the Phoenicians.

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:32.130
It was said that the dock workers wages were
“twenty dollars a day and all the Scotch

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:36.670
you could carry home,” because theft of
goods was so rampant.

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:38.530
This needed to change.

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:43.450
Malcolm McLean, a trucking company owner,
sold everything he owned to buy a ship and

00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:46.610
develop the system of containerization.

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:52.159
Unbearably simple, he designed a corrugated
steel box and created some trucks and a ship

00:01:52.159 --> 00:01:54.899
that would seamlessly hold these boxes.

00:01:54.899 --> 00:01:59.820
This connected the manufacturer straight to
the consumer.

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:04.080
Manufacturers would load these boxes, then
no hands would touch the merchandise until

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:08.920
the container was delivered to the vendor,
distributor, or consumer.

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:13.540
One of the most amazing aspects of this system
of this system it became universal.

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:18.359
We can’t even agree on currency, plug type,
DVD standard, or even which side of the road

00:02:18.359 --> 00:02:24.790
to drive on but we can agree, across the world,
on the one size of shipping container.

00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:29.890
A container loaded in Kansas will fit on the
train that takes it to the dock, then fit

00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:35.699
on the boat that takes it to China, then fit
on the truck that will take it up to Russia.

00:02:35.699 --> 00:02:39.950
Even some planes are now being designed to
fit intermodal containers.

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:45.130
Theres no need for logistics, no need for
calculations, no need for worrying if the

00:02:45.130 --> 00:02:48.210
container will work in faraway countries.

00:02:48.210 --> 00:02:52.389
But what really changed our world was how
quick this system made the loading process

00:02:52.389 --> 00:02:53.790
of a ship.

00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:58.770
What used to take more than a week could now
be done in a matter of hours.

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:02.480
Shipping costs plummeted after the introduction
of this system.

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:07.269
Whole cities, such as Newark and Oakland were
put on the map because of their new, larger

00:03:07.269 --> 00:03:11.470
ports that were needed in response to the
shipping boom.

00:03:11.470 --> 00:03:16.310
This system also helped create the global
economy that we have today, one where one

00:03:16.310 --> 00:03:20.419
car has elements from dozens of countries
across the world.

00:03:20.419 --> 00:03:25.340
Its now cheaper to manufacture many goods
on the other side of the world because shipping

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:28.100
is so inexpensive.

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:32.810
Containerization was the greatest driver of
the development of a global economy and trade

00:03:32.810 --> 00:03:33.810
network.

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:39.560
The intermodal container cut shipping time
from Europe to Australia down from 70 to 34

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:43.280
days without increasing the speed of the travel.

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:47.709
We are still witnessing the aftermath of this
innovation today.

00:03:47.709 --> 00:03:54.359
Between 1993 and 2002, the average distance
of a cargo shipment grew by 40%.

00:03:54.359 --> 00:03:59.729
This means that goods are still being manufactured
farther and farther away from their market.

00:03:59.729 --> 00:04:03.100
The value per ton of cargo is also dropping.

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:07.650
More and more cheap items are being shipped
from far away to be sold.

00:04:07.650 --> 00:04:12.180
However, many believe that containerization
could have been the last great innovation

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:13.930
in shipping.

00:04:13.930 --> 00:04:17.370
Boats can’t really go any faster while still
being profitable.

00:04:17.370 --> 00:04:22.400
15 knots is the average speed today for cargo
ships and it’s unlikely that this speed

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:25.410
will increase in the near future.

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:30.180
To make shipping faster and cheaper, one needs
to find other ways to speed up the process.

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:35.161
There are some small changes being developed,
such as the automatization of ports and the

00:04:35.161 --> 00:04:39.980
further specialization of ships, however the
greatest innovation for shipping may come

00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:42.780
from the greatest threat to mankind.

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:45.790
Global warming is opening new routes for shipping.

00:04:45.790 --> 00:04:51.010
The once frozen Northeast passage north of
Scandinavia and Russia now can be sailed on

00:04:51.010 --> 00:04:52.660
for a few months of the year.

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:58.510
In 2009, a German cargo ship became the first
commercial vessel to sail this route, and

00:04:58.510 --> 00:05:01.900
today multiple ships use this route every
year.

00:05:01.900 --> 00:05:06.790
The route shortens the shipping time between
Europe and Asia by days, avoid the pirate

00:05:06.790 --> 00:05:12.820
infested waters of the Red Sea and Gulf of
Aden, and saves on average $300,000 in fuel

00:05:12.820 --> 00:05:16.130
per vessel per voyage.

00:05:16.130 --> 00:05:17.490
So there you have it.

00:05:17.490 --> 00:05:20.410
Thats how a metal box changed our world.

00:05:20.410 --> 00:05:24.961
Without that box we wouldn’t have our phones
from China, our clothes from Bangladesh, or

00:05:24.961 --> 00:05:26.450
even our oranges from Florida.

00:05:26.450 --> 00:05:30.900
Freight shipping is the behind the scenes
process that has made our world what it is

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:31.900
today.

00:05:31.900 --> 00:05:37.080
Our economy and our lives would not be the
same without this innovation.

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:40.590
Containerization may be little known, but
its affects are more evident than almost any

00:05:40.590 --> 00:05:42.630
other invention of the 20th century.

